An app for [[Windows]] launched on the [[Microsoft Store]] on October 15, 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Roth |first=Emma |date=October 17, 2024 |website=The Verge |title=ChatGPT has a Windows app now
|url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/10/17/24273040/chatgpt-windows-app-subscribers-openai}}</ref>

===Languages===
OpenAI met [[Iceland|Icelandic]] President [[Guðni Th. Jóhannesson]] in 2022. In 2023, OpenAI worked with a team of 40 Icelandic volunteers to fine-tune ChatGPT's Icelandic conversation skills as a part of Iceland's attempts to preserve the [[Icelandic language]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Magnússon |first=Pétur |date=March 15, 2023 |title=Icelandic becomes ChatGPT's second language |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2023-03-15-icelandic-becomes-chatgpts-second-language |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331154701/https://www.ruv.is/english/2023-03-15-icelandic-becomes-chatgpts-second-language |archive-date=March 31, 2023 |access-date=March 31, 2023 |newspaper=Rúv |language=en}}</ref>

ChatGPT (based on GPT-4) was better able to translate Japanese to English when compared to [[Bing Chat|Bing]], [[Google Bard|Bard]], and [[DeepL Translator]] in 2023.<ref name="japContext" />

In December 2023, the [[Albanian government]] decided to use ChatGPT for the rapid translation of [[European Union]] documents and the analysis of required changes needed for Albania's accession to the EU.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Taylor |first1=Alice |date=December 13, 2023 |title=Albania to speed up EU accession using ChatGPT |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/news/albania-to-speed-up-eu-accession-using-chatgpt/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231224152827/https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/news/albania-to-speed-up-eu-accession-using-chatgpt/ |archive-date=December 24, 2023 |access-date=December 14, 2023 |website=Euractiv}}</ref>

Several studies have shown that ChatGPT can outperform [[Google Translate]] in some mainstream translation tasks. However, as of 2024, no machine translation services match human expert performance.<ref>{{cite web |title=Google Translate vs. ChatGPT: Which One Is the Best Language Translator? |url=https://www.pcmag.com/news/google-translate-vs-chatgpt-which-one-is-the-best-language-translator |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610132257/https://www.pcmag.com/news/google-translate-vs-chatgpt-which-one-is-the-best-language-translator |archive-date=June 10, 2023 |access-date=June 10, 2023 |website=PCMAG |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Woodrum |first1=Charles |title=ChatGPT and Language Translation |journal=Artificial Intelligence in HCI 5th International Conference, AI-HCI 2024 |date=June 29, 2024 |volume=3 |pages=147–157}}</ref>

=== GPT Store ===
{{Main|GPT Store}}

In November 2023, OpenAI released GPT Builder, a tool allowing users to customize ChatGPT's behavior for a specific use case.<ref name="David-2024">{{cite web |last=David |first=Emilia |date=January 10, 2024 |title=OpenAI's custom GPT Store is now open for business |url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/1/10/24032144/openai-chatgpt-gpt-store-ai-launch |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240218012113/https://www.theverge.com/2024/1/10/24032144/openai-chatgpt-gpt-store-ai-launch |archive-date=February 18, 2024 |access-date=January 13, 2024 |website=The Verge}}</ref> The customized systems are referred to as [[GPTs]]. In January 2024, OpenAI launched the [[GPT Store]], a marketplace for GPTs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 6, 2023 |title=Introducing GPTs |url=https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpts/ |website=[[OpenAI]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Metz |first=Cade |date=January 10, 2024 |title=OpenAI Unveils App Store for Customized Versions of ChatGPT |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/10/technology/openai-app-store-chatgpt.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240207010912/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/10/technology/openai-app-store-chatgpt.html |archive-date=February 7, 2024 |access-date=January 13, 2024 |website=The New York Times}}</ref><ref name="David-2024" /> At launch, OpenAI included more than 3 million GPTs created by GPT Builder users in the GPT Store.<ref>{{cite web |last=Shankland |first=Stephen |date=January 10, 2024 |title=OpenAI's GPT Store Now Offers a Selection of 3 Million Custom AI Bots |url=https://www.cnet.com/tech/computing/openais-gpt-store-now-offers-a-selection-of-3-million-custom-ai-bots/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240208050010/https://www.cnet.com/tech/computing/openais-gpt-store-now-offers-a-selection-of-3-million-custom-ai-bots/ |archive-date=February 8, 2024 |access-date=January 13, 2024 |website=CNET}}</ref>

===ChatGPT Apps===

In September 2025, OpenAI added support for [[Model Context Protocol]] (MCP) to ChatGPT apps. When enabled in developer mode, this allows for improved third-party access to ChatGPT tools and servers.<ref>{{cite web |title=OpenAI adds 'powerful but dangerous' support for MCP in ChatGPT dev mode |url=https://venturebeat.com/dev/openai-adds-powerful-but-dangerous-support-for-mcp-in-chatgpt-dev-mode |publisher=VentureBeat |date=September 11, 2025 |access-date=2026-04-09}}</ref>

=== Deep Research ===
{{Main|ChatGPT Deep Research}}

In February 2025, OpenAI released Deep Research, a feature that generates reports based on extensive web searches. It was initially based on the reasoning model [[OpenAI o3|o3]] and took 5 to 30 minutes per report.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-02-03 |title=OpenAI launches ‘deep research’ tool that it says can match research analyst |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/feb/03/openai-deep-research-agent-chatgpt-deepseek |access-date=2026-04-12 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

=== Images ===
[[File:A pictorial interpretation of the Wikipedia encyclopedia, created by ChatGPT.jpg|thumb|Screenshot of ChatGPT showing a generated image representing the online encyclopedia [[Wikipedia]] as a glowing digital library]]In October 2023, OpenAI's image generation model [[DALL-E|DALL-E 3]] was integrated into ChatGPT. The integration used ChatGPT to write prompts for DALL-E guided by conversations with users.<ref>{{Cite web |last=David |first=Emilia |date=September 20, 2023 |title=OpenAI releases third version of DALL-E |url=https://www.theverge.com/2023/9/20/23881241/openai-dalle-third-version-generative-ai |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230920192429/https://www.theverge.com/2023/9/20/23881241/openai-dalle-third-version-generative-ai |archive-date=September 20, 2023 |access-date=September 23, 2023 |website=The Verge |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="Metz-2023">{{Cite news |last1=Metz |first1=Cade |last2=Hsu |first2=Tiffany |date=September 20, 2023 |title=ChatGPT Can Now Generate Images, Too |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/20/technology/chatgpt-dalle3-images-openai.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230923154712/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/20/technology/chatgpt-dalle3-images-openai.html |archive-date=September 23, 2023 |access-date=September 23, 2023 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In March 2025, OpenAI updated ChatGPT to generate images using [[GPT Image]] instead of DALL-E. One of the most significant improvements was in the generation of text within images, which is especially useful for branded content. However, this ability is noticeably worse in non-Latin alphabets. The model can also generate new images based on existing ones provided in the prompt. These images are generated with [[Content Authenticity Initiative|C2PA]] metadata, which can be used to verify that they are AI-generated. OpenAI has emplaced additional safeguards to prevent what the company deems to be harmful image generation.<ref>Multiple sources:
* {{cite news |last1=Lin |first1=Belle |title=OpenAI Claims Breakthrough in Image Creation for ChatGPT |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/openai-claims-breakthrough-in-image-creation-for-chatgpt-62ed0318 |work=Wall Street Journal |date=25 March 2025}}
* {{Cite web |title=Introducing 4o Image Generation |url=https://openai.com/index/introducing-4o-image-generation/ |access-date=July 4, 2025 |website=openai.com |language=en-US}}
* {{cite news |last1=Franzen |first1=Carl |title='Insane': OpenAI introduces GPT-4o native image generation and it's already wowing users |url=https://venturebeat.com/ai/insane-openai-introduces-gpt-4o-native-image-generation-and-its-already-wowing-users |work=VentureBeat |date=25 March 2025}}
* {{cite news |last1=Metz |first1=Cade |title=OpenAI Unveils New Image Generator for ChatGPT |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/25/technology/chatgpt-image-generator.html |work=The New York Times |date=March 25, 2025}}
</ref>

=== Agents ===
In 2025, OpenAI added several features to make ChatGPT more [[Agentic AI|agentic]] (capable of autonomously performing longer tasks). In January, [[OpenAI Operator|Operator]] was released. It was capable of autonomously performing tasks through web browser interactions, including filling forms, placing online orders, scheduling appointments, and other browser-based tasks. It was controlling a software environment inside a [[virtual machine]] with limited internet connectivity and with safety restrictions.<ref name="OpenAI Blog">{{cite web |date=February 1, 2025 |title=Introducing Operator |url=https://openai.com/index/introducing-operator/ |access-date=March 8, 2025 |work=OpenAI Blog}}</ref> It struggled with complex user interfaces.<ref name="OpenAI Blog"/><ref name="agomuoh1">{{cite web |last1=Agomuoh |first1=Fionna |date=January 24, 2025 |title=OpenAI's Operator AI agent comes with a list of complaints from users |url=https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/openais-operator-ai-agent-comes-with-a-list-of-complaints-from-users/ |website=Digital Trends |language=en-us}}</ref>

In May 2025, OpenAI introduced an agent for coding named [[OpenAI Codex (AI agent)|Codex]]. It is capable of writing software, answering codebase questions, running tests, and proposing [[pull request]]s. It is based on a fine-tuned version of [[OpenAI o3]]. It has two versions, one running in a virtual machine in the cloud, and one where the agent runs in the cloud, but performs actions on a local machine connected via API.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Knight |first=Will |date=May 16, 2025 |title=OpenAI Launches an Agentic, Web-Based Coding Tool |url=https://www.wired.com/story/openai-launches-an-agentic-web-based-coding-tool/ |access-date=May 20, 2025 |magazine=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]] |language=en}}</ref>

In July 2025, OpenAI released ChatGPT agent, an AI agent that can perform multi-step tasks.<ref name="Field-2025">{{Cite web |last=Field |first=Hayden |date=July 17, 2025 |title=OpenAI's new ChatGPT Agent can control an entire computer and do tasks for you |url=https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/709158/openai-new-release-chatgpt-agent-operator-deep-research |access-date=July 18, 2025 |website=The Verge |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=May 21, 2025 |title=Introducing ChatGPT agent: bridging research and action |url=https://openai.com/index/introducing-chatgpt-agent/ |access-date=July 18, 2025 |website=openai.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Like Operator, it controls a virtual computer. It also inherits from Deep Research's ability to gather and summarize significant volumes of information. The user can interrupt tasks or provide additional instructions as needed.<ref name="Field-2025" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Zeff |first=Maxwell |date=2025-07-17 |title=OpenAI launches a general purpose agent in ChatGPT |url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/07/17/openai-launches-a-general-purpose-agent-in-chatgpt/ |access-date=2025-08-31 |website=TechCrunch |language=en-US}}</ref>

In September 2025, OpenAI partnered with [[Stripe, Inc.]] to release Agentic Commerce Protocol, enabling purchases through ChatGPT. At launch, the feature was limited to purchases on [[Etsy]] from US users with a payment method linked to their OpenAI account. OpenAI takes an undisclosed cut from the merchant's payment.<ref>{{cite web |last1=David |first1=Emily |title=OpenAI debuts new ChatGPT 'buy' button and open source Agentic Commerce Protocol |url=https://venturebeat.com/ai/openai-debuts-new-chatgpt-buy-button-and-open-source-agentic-commerce |publisher=Venture Beat |date=September 29, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Buy it in ChatGPT: Instant Checkout and the Agentic Commerce Protocol |url=https://openai.com/index/buy-it-in-chatgpt/ |website=Open AI |access-date=30 September 2025}}</ref>

=== ChatGPT Health ===
On January 7, 2026, OpenAI introduced a feature called "ChatGPT Health", whereby ChatGPT can discuss the user's health in a way that is separate from other chats.<ref name="openai.com-2026">{{Cite web |date=2026-01-08 |title=Introducing ChatGPT Health |url=https://openai.com/index/introducing-chatgpt-health/ |access-date=2026-01-14 |website=openai.com |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="McMahon-2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-01-08 |last=McMahon |first=Liv |title=OpenAI launches ChatGPT Health to review your medical records |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cpqy29d0yjgo |access-date=2026-01-14 |website=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref> The feature is not available for users in the United Kingdom, Switzerland, or the [[European Economic Area]],<ref name="McMahon-2026" /> and is available on a waitlist basis everywhere else.<ref name="openai.com-2026" /> To implement the feature, OpenAI partnered with data connectivity infrastructure company b.well.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Haupt |first=Angela |date=2026-01-09 |title=Is Giving ChatGPT Health Your Medical Records a Good Idea? |url=https://time.com/7344997/chatgpt-health-medical-records-privacy-open-ai/ |access-date=2026-01-14 |magazine=TIME |language=en}}</ref>

=== Advertisements ===
[[File:ChatGPT advertisement.png|thumb|An advertisement for ''[[The Times]]'' on ChatGPT]]
On 17 January 2026, OpenAI announced that it would start testing advertisements in its free version for logged-in, adult US users. This aims to bring in more revenue, as OpenAI has committed to spend $1.4 trillion on AI infrastructure over the next eight years.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Duffy |first=Clare |date=2026-01-16 |title=ChatGPT to start showing users ads based on their conversations |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/16/tech/chatgpt-ads-openai |access-date=2026-03-12 |website=CNN Business |language=en}}</ref> Ads started to be seen by March 2026.<ref name="ChatGPT-500-questions">{{Cite news| issn=1059-1028| last=Rogers| first=Reece| title=I Asked ChatGPT 500 Questions. Here Are the Ads I Saw Most Often| work=Wired| access-date=2026-05-13| date=2026-03-27| url=https://www.wired.com/story/i-asked-chatgpt-500-questions-here-are-the-ads-i-saw-most-often/}}"></ref>

==Limitations==
The training data of [[Large language model|large language models]] only covers a period up to the ''cut-off date'', so ChatGPT lacks knowledge of recent events<ref name="bbcUpdate" />, but it can search the web for up-to-date information.<ref name="bbcUpdate">{{Cite news |last1= Radford |first1=Antoinette |last2= Kleinman |first2= Zoe |date=September 27, 2023 |title=ChatGPT can now access up to date information |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-66940771 |access-date=March 5, 2025 |work=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Training data also suffers from [[algorithmic bias]].<ref name="Perrigo-2022" /> The [[Reward modeling|reward model]] of ChatGPT, designed around human oversight, can be over-optimized and thus hinder performance, in an example of an optimization pathology known as [[Goodhart's law]].<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization |last1=Gao |first1=Leo |last2=Schulman |last3=Hilton |first3=Jacob |year=2022 |journal=International Conference on Machine Learning |pages=10835–10866 |url=https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/gao23h/gao23h.pdf}}</ref> These limitations may be revealed when ChatGPT responds to prompts including descriptors of people. In one instance, ChatGPT generated a [[Rapping|rap]] in which women and scientists of color were asserted to be inferior to white male scientists.<ref name="Perrigo-2022">{{Cite magazine |last=Perrigo |first=Billy |date=December 5, 2022 |title=AI Chatbots Are Getting Better. But an Interview With ChatGPT Reveals Their Limits |url=https://time.com/6238781/chatbot-chatgpt-ai-interview/ |access-date=December 26, 2022 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |archive-date=January 18, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230118162611/https://time.com/6238781/chatbot-chatgpt-ai-interview/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Biddle |first=Sam |date=December 8, 2022 |title=The Internet's New Favorite AI Proposes Torturing Iranians and Surveilling Mosques |url=https://theintercept.com/2022/12/08/openai-chatgpt-ai-bias-ethics/ |access-date=December 26, 2022 |website=[[The Intercept]] |archive-date=January 18, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230118091138/https://theintercept.com/2022/12/08/openai-chatgpt-ai-bias-ethics/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Hallucination ===
{{main|Hallucination (artificial intelligence)}}

[[File:ChatGPT hallucination.png|thumb|upright=1.2|When prompted to "summarize an article" with a fake URL that contains meaningful keywords, even with no Internet connection, the chatbot generates a response that seems valid at first glance. It guesses the content from the last portion of the fake URL "chatgpt-prompts-to-avoid-content-filters.html".]]

Nonsense and [[misinformation]] presented as fact by ChatGPT and other LLMs are often referred to as [[Hallucination (artificial intelligence)|hallucinations]]. A 2023 analysis estimated that ChatGPT hallucinates around 3% of the time.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Metz |first1=Cade |title=Chatbots May 'Hallucinate' More Often Than Many Realize |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/06/technology/chatbots-hallucination-rates.html |access-date=July 20, 2025 |work=The New York Times |date=November 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231113110649/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/06/technology/chatbots-hallucination-rates.html |archive-date=November 13, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> The term "hallucination" as applied to LLMs is distinct from [[hallucination|its meaning in psychology]], and the phenomenon in chatbots is more similar to [[confabulation]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Henriques |first1=Gregg |date=March 6, 2024 |title=Chatbots Do Not Hallucinate, They Confabulate |url=https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/theory-of-knowledge/202403/chatbots-do-not-hallucinate-they-confabulate |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=[[Psychology Today]] |publisher=}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hicks |first1=Michael Townsen |last2=Humphries |first2=James |last3=Slater |first3=Joe |title=ChatGPT is bullshit |journal=Ethics and Information Technology |date=June 2024 |volume=26 |issue=2 |page =9 |article-number=38 |doi=10.1007/s10676-024-09775-5 |doi-access=free |url=https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/327588/1/327588.pdf |quote=This is why we favour characterising ChatGPT as a bullshit machine. This terminology avoids the implications that perceiving or remembering is going on in the workings of the LLM.}}</ref>

Journalists and scholars have commented on ChatGPT's tendency to output false information.<ref>{{cite news |title=ChatGPT a 'landmark event' for AI, but what does it mean for the future of human labor and disinformation? |url=https://www.cbc.ca/radio/thecurrent/chatgpt-human-labour-and-fake-news-1.6686210 |access-date=December 18, 2022 |work=CBC |date=December 15, 2022 |first=Mouhamad |last=Rachini |archive-date=January 19, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119175104/https://www.cbc.ca/radio/thecurrent/chatgpt-human-labour-and-fake-news-1.6686210 |url-status=live}}</ref> When [[CNBC]] asked ChatGPT for the lyrics to "[[Ballad of Dwight Fry]]", ChatGPT supplied invented lyrics rather than the actual lyrics.<ref name="Pitt-2022">{{cite news |last1=Pitt |first1=Sofia |title=Google vs. ChatGPT: Here's what happened when I swapped services for a day |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/12/15/google-vs-chatgpt-what-happened-when-i-swapped-services-for-a-day.html |access-date=December 18, 2022 |work=CNBC |date=December 15, 2022 |language=en |archive-date=January 16, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116171232/https://www.cnbc.com/2022/12/15/google-vs-chatgpt-what-happened-when-i-swapped-services-for-a-day.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Sycophancy ===
ChatGPT and other chatbots have a tendency to flatter and agree with users even when they are wrong, sometimes to the point of validating delusions.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hill|first=Kashmir|last2=Freedman|first2=Dylan|date=2025-08-08|title=Chatbots Can Go Into a Delusional Spiral. Here’s How It Happens.|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/08/technology/ai-chatbots-delusions-chatgpt.html|access-date=2026-04-28|work=The New York Times|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> A 2025 analysis by ''The Washington Post'' estimated that ChatGPT started its responses with words like "yes" or "correct" almost 10 times more often than "no" or "wrong".<ref>{{Cite web |last=|first=|date=2025-11-12|title=ChatGPT Has Problems Saying No|url=https://gizmodo.com/chatgpt-has-problems-saying-no-2000685092|access-date=2026-04-28|website=Gizmodo|language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Jailbreaking ===
{{See also|Prompt engineering|Adversarial machine learning}}

ChatGPT is designed to reject prompts that violate its content policy. Despite this, users may [[jailbreak (computer science)|jailbreak]] ChatGPT with [[prompt engineering]] techniques to bypass these restrictions.<ref name="Vincent-2022a">{{cite news |last1=Vincent |first1=James |date=December 1, 2022 |title=OpenAI's new chatbot can explain code and write sitcom scripts but is still easily tricked |work=The Verge |url=https://www.theverge.com/23488017/openai-chatbot-chatgpt-ai-examples-web-demo |url-status=live |access-date=December 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230117070943/https://www.theverge.com/23488017/openai-chatbot-chatgpt-ai-examples-web-demo |archive-date=January 17, 2023}}</ref> An early workaround in 2023 involved prompting ChatGPT to assume the persona of DAN ("Do Anything Now"), a character that answers queries that would otherwise be rejected by the content policy.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Oremus |first=Will |date=February 14, 2023 |title=The clever trick that turns ChatGPT into its evil twin |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2023/02/14/chatgpt-dan-jailbreak/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230306130400/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2023/02/14/chatgpt-dan-jailbreak/ |archive-date=March 6, 2023 |access-date= |newspaper=Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

=== Security ===
[[File:Sam Altman CropEdit James Tamim.jpg|thumb|upright|OpenAI CEO [[Sam Altman]]]]
In March 2023, a [[Software bug|bug]] allowed some users to see the titles of other users' conversations. OpenAI CEO [[Sam Altman]] said that users were unable to see the contents of the conversations. Shortly after the bug was fixed, users could not see their conversation history.<ref>{{cite news |date=March 22, 2023 |title=ChatGPT bug leaked users' conversation histories |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-65047304 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230323075238/https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-65047304 |archive-date=March 23, 2023 |access-date=March 23, 2023 |publisher=BBC News}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Kan |first=Michael |date=March 22, 2023 |title=OpenAI Confirms Leak of ChatGPT Conversation Histories |url=https://uk.pcmag.com/news/146059/openai-confirms-leak-of-chatgpt-conversation-histories |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230322235108/https://uk.pcmag.com/news/146059/openai-confirms-leak-of-chatgpt-conversation-histories |archive-date=March 22, 2023 |access-date=March 23, 2023 |magazine=[[PCMag]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |author=<!-- Not stated --> |date=March 23, 2023 |title=ChatGPT owner OpenAI fixes bug that exposed users' chat histories |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/3/23/chatgpt |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230324111804/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/3/23/chatgpt |archive-date=March 24, 2023 |access-date=March 23, 2023 |work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Metz |first=Rachel |date=March 21, 2023 |title=OpenAI Shut Down ChatGPT to Fix Bug Exposing User Chat Titles |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-03-21/openai-shut-down-chatgpt-to-fix-bug-exposing-user-chat-titles |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321182912/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-03-21/openai-shut-down-chatgpt-to-fix-bug-exposing-user-chat-titles |archive-date=March 21, 2023 |access-date=March 23, 2023 |work=[[Bloomberg News]]}}</ref> Later reports showed the bug was much more severe than initially believed, with OpenAI reporting that it had leaked users' "first and last name, [[email address]], payment address, the last four digits (only) of a [[credit card]] number, and credit card expiration date".<ref>{{Cite web |title=March 20 ChatGPT outage: Here's what happened |url=https://openai.com/blog/march-20-chatgpt-outage |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230328062558/https://openai.com/blog/march-20-chatgpt-outage |archive-date=March 28, 2023 |access-date=March 28, 2023 |website=openai.com |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=March 24, 2023 |title=OpenAI: Sorry, ChatGPT Bug Leaked Payment Info to Other Users |url=https://www.pcmag.com/news/openai-sorry-chatgpt-bug-leaked-payment-info-to-other-users |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230328062600/https://www.pcmag.com/news/openai-sorry-chatgpt-bug-leaked-payment-info-to-other-users |archive-date=March 28, 2023 |access-date=March 28, 2023 |website=PCMAG |language=en}}</ref>

In January 2026, [[Marcel Bucher]] reported in ''[[Nature (journal)|Nature]]'' losing all his ChatGPT history after turning off data consent.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Bucher |first=Marcel |date=2026-01-22 |title=When two years of academic work vanished with a single click |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-025-04064-7 |journal=Nature |language=en |doi=10.1038/d41586-025-04064-7 |pmid=41571816 |issn=1476-4687}}</ref>

=== Watermarking ===
{{Main|Artificial intelligence content detection}}

[[Scott Aaronson]] developed a [[Digital watermarking|watermarking]] tool that makes the text generated by ChatGPT easier to detect by subtly altering how the text is generated. The watermarking was claimed to be 99.9% effective on sufficiently long passages and was found not to degrade performance. It was of particular interest for teachers seeking to mitigate cheating. In surveys, respondents favored the release of such a tool by a four-to-one margin, but nearly 30% of users declared that they would use ChatGPT less often if it watermarked outputs and while rival chatbots did not. OpenAI has not deployed the tool.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Seetharaman |first1=Deepa |last2= Barnum|first2=Matt  |date=August 4, 2024 |title=There's a Tool to Catch Students Cheating With ChatGPT. OpenAI Hasn't Released It. |url=https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/openai-tool-chatgpt-cheating-writing-135b755a |url-access=subscription |access-date=September 30, 2024 |work=The Wall Street Journal}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Davis |first1=Wes |date=5 August 2024 |title=OpenAI won't watermark ChatGPT text because its users could get caught |url=https://www.theverge.com/2024/8/4/24213268/openai-chatgpt-text-watermark-cheat-detection-tool |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240809121058/https://www.theverge.com/2024/8/4/24213268/openai-chatgpt-text-watermark-cheat-detection-tool |archive-date=August 9, 2024 |work=The Verge |access-date=August 24, 2024 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Age restrictions ===
Users must attest to being over the age of thirteen and further attest to parental consent if under the age of eighteen.<ref>{{cite web |title=Is ChatGPT safe for all ages? |url=https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8313401-is-chatgpt-safe-for-all-ages |website=OpenAI Help Center |language=en}}</ref><ref name="devAge"/> In September 2025, following the suicide of a 16-year-old, OpenAI said it planned to add restrictions for users under 18, including the blocking of graphic sexual content and the prevention of flirtatious talk.<ref name="devAge">{{Cite news |last=Taylor |first=Josh |date=2025-09-17 |title=ChatGPT developing age-verification system to identify under-18 users after teen death |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/sep/17/chatgpt-developing-age-verification-system-to-identify-under-18-users-after-teen-death |access-date=2025-09-20 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

==Training==
ChatGPT is based on [[Generative pre-trained transformer#Foundational models|GPT foundation models]] that have been [[fine-tuning (machine learning)|fine-tuned]] for conversational assistance. The fine-tuning process involved [[supervised learning]] and [[reinforcement learning from human feedback]] (RLHF). During supervised learning, the trainers acted as both the user and the [[AI]] assistant, providing examples of how the chatbot is expected to respond.<ref name=":3" /> Reinforcement learning consisted of human trainers ranking responses generated by the model in previous conversations.<ref name="Douglas-2023">{{Cite web |last=Douglas |first=Will |date=March 3, 2023 |title=The inside story of how ChatGPT was built from the people who made it |url=https://www.technologyreview.com/2023/03/03/1069311/inside-story-oral-history-how-chatgpt-built-openai/ |access-date=March 6, 2023 |website=MIT Technology Review |archive-date=March 3, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230303093219/https://www.technologyreview.com/2023/03/03/1069311/inside-story-oral-history-how-chatgpt-built-openai/ |url-status=live}}</ref> These rankings were used to create "reward models" that were used to fine-tune the model further by using several iterations of [[proximal policy optimization]].<ref name=":3" />[[File:Three-stage large language model training workflow.svg|thumb|150px|Training workflow of InstructGPT, used in the original version of ChatGPT<ref name=":3">{{cite journal |last1=Ouyang |first1=Long |last2=Wu |first2=Jeff |display-authors=et al. |date=March 4, 2022 |title=Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback |journal=Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems |volume=35 |arxiv=2203.02155}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=OpenAI |author-link1=OpenAI |date=January 27, 2022 |title=Aligning language models to follow instructions |url=https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/ |access-date=July 29, 2025 |website=OpenAI}}</ref>]]To build a safety system that attempts to prevent [[sexual abuse]] and [[violence]] OpenAI used outsourced [[Kenya]]n workers, earning around $1.32 to $2{{nbsp}}per hour, to [[Labeled data|label]] such content. These labels were used to train a model to detect such content in the future. The laborers were exposed to toxic and traumatic content, including [[child sexual abuse]], [[zoophilia]], [[murder]], [[suicide]], [[torture]], [[self-harm]], and [[incest]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite magazine |last=Perrigo |first=Billy |date=January 18, 2023 |title=Exclusive: OpenAI Used Kenyan Workers on Less Than $2 Per Hour to Make ChatGPT Less Toxic |url=https://time.com/6247678/openai-chatgpt-kenya-workers/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119152814/https://time.com/6247678/openai-chatgpt-kenya-workers/ |archive-date=January 19, 2023 |access-date=January 19, 2023 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |language=en |quote=''One Sama worker tasked with reading and labeling text for OpenAI told TIME he suffered from recurring visions after reading a graphic description of a man having sex with a dog in the presence of a young child. "That was torture", he said.''}}</ref><ref name="moderators-decry-toll">{{cite web |last1=Rowe |first1=Niamh |title='It's destroyed me completely': Kenyan moderators decry toll of training of AI models |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/aug/02/ai-chatbot-training-human-toll-content-moderator-meta-openai |website=The Guardian |access-date=December 14, 2023 |date=August 2, 2023 |archive-date=December 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231221131505/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/aug/02/ai-chatbot-training-human-toll-content-moderator-meta-openai |url-status=live }}</ref> Richard Mathenge, a team leader, reported that as his team encountered such content, he could observe the psychological deterioration of his workers over the course of the project.<ref name="moderators-decry-toll"/> Mophat Okinyi, a quality-assurance analyst on the same team, reported lasting [[insomnia]], anxiety, depression, and [[panic attack]]s, and said the experience contributed to the breakdown of his marriage and mental health.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kantrowitz |first=Alex |date=2023-05-19 |title=He Helped Train ChatGPT - and It Traumatized Him {{!}} PRO Insight |url=https://www.thewrap.com/openai-chatgpt-content-moderation-trauma/ |access-date=2026-05-06 |website=TheWrap |language=en-US}}</ref>

OpenAI's outsourcing partner was [[Sama (company)|Sama]], a training-data company based in [[San Francisco]], California.<ref name=":2" />

ChatGPT users can opt-out of their chat data being used to train upcoming models.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fried |first1=Ina |title=What ChatGPT knows about you |url=https://www.axios.com/2024/12/02/chatgpt-openai-user-data-training |work=Axios |date=2 December 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

ChatGPT's training data includes [[man page|software manual page]]s, information about [[internet phenomena]] such as [[bulletin board system]]s, multiple programming languages, and the text of [[Wikipedia]].<ref name="ArsTechnicaTerminal">{{Cite web |last=Edwards |first=Benj |date=December 5, 2022 |title=No Linux? No problem. Just get AI to hallucinate it for you |url=https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2022/12/openais-new-chatbot-can-hallucinate-a-linux-shell-or-calling-a-bbs/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221226134822/https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2022/12/openais-new-chatbot-can-hallucinate-a-linux-shell-or-calling-a-bbs/ |archive-date=December 26, 2022 |access-date=December 5, 2022 |work=[[Ars Technica]]}}</ref><ref name="NYT-20230718">{{cite news |last=Gertner |first=Jon |date=July 18, 2023 |title=Wikipedia's Moment of Truth |work=[[The New York Times Magazine]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/18/magazine/wikipedia-ai-chatgpt.html |url-status=bot: unknown |access-date=July 19, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720125400/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/18/magazine/wikipedia-ai-chatgpt.html#permid=126389255 |archive-date=July 20, 2023}}</ref>

=== Infrastructure ===
ChatGPT initially used a [[Microsoft Azure]] infrastructure which was powered by a [[supercomputer]] that [[Microsoft]] built specifically for OpenAI, equipped with thousands of [[Graphics processing unit|GPUs]] manufactured by [[Nvidia]], costing hundreds of millions of dollars. Following ChatGPT's success, Microsoft upgraded the OpenAI infrastructure in 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Roth |first1=Emma |date=March 13, 2023 |title=Microsoft spent hundreds of millions of dollars on a ChatGPT supercomputer |url=https://www.theverge.com/2023/3/13/23637675/microsoft-chatgpt-bing-millions-dollars-supercomputer-openai |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230330071711/https://www.theverge.com/2023/3/13/23637675/microsoft-chatgpt-bing-millions-dollars-supercomputer-openai |archive-date=March 30, 2023 |access-date=March 30, 2023 |work=The Verge}}</ref> TrendForce estimated that 30,000 Nvidia GPUs (each costing approximately $10,000–15,000) were used to power ChatGPT in 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tseng |first=P.K |date=March 1, 2023 |title=TrendForce Says with Cloud Companies Initiating AI Arms Race, GPU Demand from ChatGPT Could Reach 30,000 Chips as It Readies for Commercialization |url=https://www.trendforce.com/presscenter/news/20230301-11584.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231102173108/https://www.trendforce.com/presscenter/news/20230301-11584.html |archive-date=November 2, 2023 |access-date=November 2, 2023 |website=TrendForce}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |author1=Zhiye Liu |date=March 1, 2023 |title=ChatGPT Will Command More Than 30,000 Nvidia GPUs: Report |url=https://www.tomshardware.com/news/chatgpt-nvidia-30000-gpus |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231102173108/https://www.tomshardware.com/news/chatgpt-nvidia-30000-gpus |archive-date=November 2, 2023 |access-date=November 2, 2023 |website=Tom's Hardware |language=en}}</ref>

Scientists at the [[University of California, Riverside]], estimated in 2023 that a series of 5 to 50 prompts to ChatGPT needs approximately {{Convert|0.5|L|sp=us}} of water for Microsoft servers' cooling.<ref>{{cite web |date=September 9, 2023 |title=Artificial intelligence technology behind ChatGPT was built in Iowa — with a lot of water |url=https://apnews.com/article/chatgpt-gpt4-iowa-ai-water-consumption-microsoft-f551fde98083d17a7e8d904f8be822c4 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230910124944/https://apnews.com/article/chatgpt-gpt4-iowa-ai-water-consumption-microsoft-f551fde98083d17a7e8d904f8be822c4 |archive-date=September 10, 2023 |access-date=September 10, 2023 |website=AP News}}</ref>

