* {{cite journal |last1=Endo |first1=Yutaka |last2=Sasaki |first2=Kazunari |last3=Moazzam |first3=Zorays |last4=Lima |first4=Henrique A. |last5=Schenk |first5=Austin |last6=Limkemann |first6=Ashley |last7=Washburn |first7=Kenneth |last8=Pawlik |first8=Timothy M. |title=Quality of ChatGPT Responses to Questions Related To Liver Transplantation |journal=Journal of Gastrointestinal Surgery |date=August 1, 2023 |volume=27 |issue=8 |pages=1716–1719 |doi=10.1007/s11605-023-05714-9 |pmid=37254022 |url=https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11605-023-05714-9 |language=en |issn=1873-4626|url-access=subscription }}
* {{cite journal |last1=Tan |first1=Songtao |last2=Xin |first2=Xin |last3=Wu |first3=Di |title=ChatGPT in medicine: prospects and challenges: a review article |journal=International Journal of Surgery |date=June 2024 |volume=110 |issue=6 |pages=3701–3706 |doi=10.1097/JS9.0000000000001312 |pmid=38502861 |pmc=11175750 }}
* {{cite journal |last1=Liu |first1=Hilary Y. |last2=Alessandri Bonetti |first2=Mario |last3=De Lorenzi |first3=Francesca |last4=Gimbel |first4=Michael L. |last5=Nguyen |first5=Vu T. |last6=Egro |first6=Francesco M. |title=Consulting the Digital Doctor: Google Versus ChatGPT as Sources of Information on Breast Implant-Associated Anaplastic Large Cell Lymphoma and Breast Implant Illness |journal=Aesthetic Plastic Surgery |date=February 2024 |volume=48 |issue=4 |pages=590–607 |doi=10.1007/s00266-023-03713-4 |pmid=37903939 |issn=1432-5241}}</ref> and assist professionals with diagnosis and staying up to date with clinical guidelines.<ref name="medical bundle 1">{{cite journal |last1=Lewandowski |first1=Miłosz |last2=Łukowicz |first2=Paweł |last3=Świetlik |first3=Dariusz |last4=Barańska-Rybak |first4=Wioletta |title=ChatGPT-3.5 and ChatGPT-4 dermatological knowledge level based on the Specialty Certificate Examination in Dermatology |journal=Clinical and Experimental Dermatology |date=June 25, 2024 |volume=49 |issue=7 |pages=686–691 |doi=10.1093/ced/llad255 |pmid=37540015 |url=https://academic.oup.com/ced/article/49/7/686/7237242|doi-access=free }}</ref> It can be used to summarize medical journal articles for researchers. In medical education, it can explain concepts, generate case scenarios, and be used by students preparing for licensing examinations.<ref name="medmeta2024" />

A February 2023 study in ''[[PLOS Digital Health]]'' found that ChatGPT 3.5 was capable of passing the [[United States Medical Licensing Examination]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kung |first1=Tiffany H. |last2=Cheatham |first2=Morgan |last3=Medenilla |first3=Arielle |last4=Sillos |first4=Czarina |last5=De Leon |first5=Lorie |last6=Elepaño |first6=Camille |last7=Madriaga |first7=Maria |last8=Aggabao |first8=Rimel |last9=Diaz-Candido |first9=Giezel |last10=Maningo |first10=James |last11=Tseng |first11=Victor |title=Performance of ChatGPT on USMLE: Potential for AI-assisted medical education using large language models |journal=PLOS Digital Health |date=9 February 2023 |volume=2 |issue=2 |article-number= e0000198|doi=10.1371/journal.pdig.0000198 |doi-access=free |pmid=36812645 |pmc=9931230 }}</ref><ref name="DePeau-Wilson-2023">{{Cite news |last=DePeau-Wilson |first=Michael |date=January 19, 2023 |title=AI Passes U.S. Medical Licensing Exam |work=MedPage Today |url=https://www.medpagetoday.com/special-reports/exclusives/102705 |access-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-date=April 9, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230409211356/https://www.medpagetoday.com/special-reports/exclusives/102705 |url-status=live}}</ref> ChatGPT has also passed the Specialty Certificate Examination in Dermatology.<ref name="medical bundle 1" />

However, ChatGPT shows inconsistent responses, lack of specificity, lack of control over patient data, and a limited ability to take additional context (such as regional variations) into consideration.<ref name="medmeta2024">{{cite journal |last1=Tan |first1=Songtao |last2=Xin |first2=Xin |last3=Wu |first3=Di |title=ChatGPT in medicine: prospects and challenges: a review article |journal=International Journal of Surgery |date=June 2024 |volume=110 |issue=6 |pages=3701–3706 |doi=10.1097/JS9.0000000000001312 |pmid=38502861 |pmc=11175750 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Howard |first1=Alex |last2=Hope |first2=William |last3=Gerada |first3=Alessandro |date=April 2023 |title=ChatGPT and antimicrobial advice: the end of the consulting infection doctor? |url=https://www.thelancet.com/journals/laninf/article/PIIS1473-3099(23)00113-5/fulltext#back-bib1 |journal=The Lancet Infectious Diseases |volume=23 |issue=4 |pages=405–406 |doi=10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00113-5 |pmid=36822213 |s2cid=257072872 |issn=1473-3099 |access-date=May 2, 2023 |archive-date=March 25, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230325232830/https://www.thelancet.com/journals/laninf/article/PIIS1473-3099(23)00113-5/fulltext#back-bib1 |url-status=live|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The [[Hallucination (artificial intelligence)|hallucinations]] characteristic of LLMs pose particular danger in medical contexts, and ChatGPT's ability to come up with false or faulty citations was highly criticized.<ref name="medmeta2024" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gravel |first1=Jocelyn |last2=D'Amours-Gravel |first2=Madeleine |last3=Osmanlliu |first3=Esli |date=September 1, 2023 |title=Learning to Fake It: Limited Responses and Fabricated References Provided by ChatGPT for Medical Questions |journal=Mayo Clinic Proceedings: Digital Health |language=en |volume=1 |issue=3 |pages=226–234 |doi=10.1016/j.mcpdig.2023.05.004 |issn=2949-7612 |doi-access=free |pmid=40206627 |pmc=11975740 }}</ref> According to a 2024 study in the ''[[International Journal of Surgery]]'', concerns include "research fraud, lack of originality, ethics, copyright, legal difficulties".<ref name="medmeta2024"/>

==== Mental health ====
{{see also|Artificial intelligence in mental health|Chatbot psychosis}}
According to a September 2025 article in ''[[Lancet Psychiatry]]'', many individuals use ChatGPT and comparable chatbots for mental health and emotional support despite a warning from OpenAI against using ChatGPT as a therapist. The study notes a lack of research on efficacy, poor consistency in dangerous situations, limited regulation and liability, and poor transparency from OpenAI.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Rousmaniere |first1=Tony |last2=Goldberg |first2=Simon B |last3=Torous |first3=John |date= 2026|title=Large language models as mental health providers |journal=The Lancet Psychiatry |language=en |volume=13 |issue=1 |pages=7–9 |doi=10.1016/S2215-0366(25)00269-X |pmid=40939602 |pmc=12462653 }}</ref>

A July 2025 study in the journal ''Digital Health'' found that users reported employing ChatGPT to manage mental health concerns "due to perceived therapist-like qualities (e.g. emotional support, accurate understanding, and constructive feedback) and machine-like benefits (e.g. constant availability, expansive cognitive capacity, lack of negative reactions, and perceived objectivity)." The study calls for improved [[AI literacy]] and mandatory disclosure from AI providers to address ethical concerns such as privacy, bias, the lack of liability, and emotional over-reliance.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Luo |first1=Xiaochen |last2=Ghosh |first2=Smita |last3=Tilley |first3=Jacqueline L. |last4=Besada |first4=Patrica |last5=Wang |first5=Jinqiu |last6=Xiang |first6=Yangyang |date=2025 |title="Shaping ChatGPT into my Digital Therapist": A thematic analysis of social media discourse on using generative artificial intelligence for mental health |journal=Digital Health |publisher=Sage Journals |volume=11 |article-number=20552076251351088 |doi=10.1177/20552076251351088 |issn=2055-2076 |pmc=12254646 |pmid=40656852}}</ref>

